Genomic Prediction of Complex Traits (GPCT01) ? Applications for Ecology

Genomic Prediction of Complex Traits (GPCT01)<https://prstats.org/course/genomic-prediction-of-complex-traits-gpct01/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>

Delivered by Dr. Javier Fern?ndez-Gonz?lez, a quantitative geneticist and biostatistician specialising in statistical genomics, quantitative genetics, genomic prediction, variance-component estimation, and the statistical analysis of complex traits.

Learn how to use genome-wide genetic and phenotypic data to analyse and predict complex traits, with approaches relevant to ecological genetics, evolutionary ecology, conservation genomics, and studies of genotype?environment relationships.

Many ecologically important characteristics ? including growth, morphology, physiology, phenology, stress tolerance, disease resistance, reproductive traits, and responses to environmental conditions ? are complex quantitative traitsinfluenced by many genetic variants together with environmental effects. Genomic prediction provides a framework for investigating this complexity by combining genome-wide marker and phenotypic data to quantify genetic effects, estimate heritability, predict phenotypes, and explore how genetic and environmental factors contribute to biological variation.

What you'll gain

  *   An understanding of the fundamental principles of genomic prediction
  *   Insight into the factors influencing genomic prediction accuracy
  *   A practical understanding of linear models, fixed and random effects, covariance structures, REML, and shrinkage
  *   Experience with genomic prediction models including GBLUP and rrBLUP
  *   Skills constructing and interpreting genomic relationship matrices
  *   Methods for estimating heritability
  *   Approaches for estimating additive and non-additive genetic effects
  *   Understanding of single-stage and multi-stage modelling
  *   Introduction to Bayesian and machine-learning alternatives
  *   Practical experience preparing phenotypic and genotypic data
  *   Understanding of cross-validation for evaluating predictive performance
  *   Introduction to multi-trait genomic prediction
  *   Approaches for modelling genotype-by-environment interactions

Course format

  *   5-day live, instructor-led online course
  *   22.5 hours of training
  *   Combination of lectures and hands-on practical exercises
  *   Practical implementation using realistic genomic datasets
  *   Opportunity to discuss participants' own data
  *   All code, datasets, and presentation materials provided
  *   All sessions recorded
  *   30 days of recording access and post-course email support

Who is this course for?

  *   Ecologists and evolutionary ecologists working with genomic data
  *   Molecular ecologists
  *   Conservation geneticists and genomic researchers
  *   Quantitative and population geneticists
  *   Researchers studying complex ecological traits
  *   Researchers investigating genotype-by-environment interactions
  *   Researchers combining genomic, phenotypic, and environmental data
  *   Postgraduate students and early-career researchers

Participants should have a basic understanding of quantitative and population genetics, molecular biology, statistics, and matrix algebra, together with some familiarity with R.

Why take this course?

Ecologists increasingly use genomic information to understand why individuals and populations differ in ecologically important traits and how those traits respond to environmental variation. Many such traits are highly polygenic, meaning they cannot be adequately explained by a small number of individual genetic markers.

Genomic prediction offers a different approach by using information distributed across the genome. This can help researchers quantify genetic contributions to phenotypic variation, estimate heritability, predict complex traits, and investigate the relative contributions of genetic and environmental factors.

Of particular relevance to ecological research, the course introduces genotype-by-environment modelling, providing a framework for examining how genetic effects vary across environmental conditions. It also covers multi-trait models, additive and non-additive genetic effects, genomic relationships, and alternative Bayesian and machine-learning approaches.

Whether you're investigating local adaptation, phenotypic variation, environmental responses, quantitative traits, conservation genetics, or genotype?environment interactions, this course provides a practical foundation for incorporating genomic prediction into ecological and evolutionary research.

